# 🐶 Dog Activity Tracker (ESP32-CAM)

This project is a modular firmware designed to detect and log different types of dog activity using simulated motion data on an ESP32-CAM. The idea was to mimic a real-world pet activity tracker — but using fake sensor values to test the logic.

## 📦 Project Overview

Each module focuses on a specific milestone:

| Milestone | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| 1.1 | Simulated QMI8658 accelerometer with fake motion data |
| 1.2 | Implemented deep sleep, RTC memory, and optional 2-min timer wake |
| 1.3 | RTC-based time management and NTP sync |
| 1.4 | Classifies activity into Resting, Walking, Running, Playing using thresholds |
| 1.5 | Logs data every 10 minutes to LittleFS with timestamped binary-like format |


## ⚠️ Challenges Faced

- I didn't have a proper USB-to-Serial programmer for the ESP32-CAM
- Tried using a Raspberry Pi Pico as a serial flasher... many, many times.
- The board powered up, but never got past the “connecting…” upload stage — probably due to boot mode or reset issues.
- So unfortunately, I wasn’t able to run the code on real hardware.

## ✅ What Was Done

- I got the full logic and structure of the firmware working — including motion detection, activity classification, sleep modes, and data logging.
- Used serial print statements to simulate output and validate that the right functions were being triggered.
- There's also a #define TEST_MODE you can enable in the code — it simulates motion automatically so you can see the output even without real sensor input.

## 💻 How to Simulate (if ESP32 is available)

1. Connect your ESP32-CAM with a USB-to-Serial adapter.
2. Clone this repo and open it with VSCode + PlatformIO.
3. Build the project.
4. Upload to ESP32 and open the serial monitor.
5. Observe simulated activity detection and logging every 10 seconds.

## 📂 Sample Output?

Unfortunately, due to flashing failure, no live serial logs could be captured. However, the firmware is designed to print clean classification and logging messages once it's running on actual hardware.
But the firmware is designed to print clear logs like:
  [Activity] Running
  [Logger] Logged 10-minute data chunk to /data/YYYYMMDD.dat


